VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-048
Reason for Visit: Acute severe panic attack, palpitations, and generalized weakness triggered by researching medical conditions online.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, severe chest tightness, tachycardia, and dyspnea. Immediate clinical review was mandatory to rule out acute myocardial infarction, pulmonary embolism, or an acute severe autoimmune cardiopulmonary complication prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My chest is incredibly tight, I can't catch a full breath, and my heart is skipping beats. I was reading about connective tissue diseases and I'm convinced my lungs and heart are failing.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports clinical features lacking specific diagnostic criteria, historically manifesting as mild fatigue. Tonight, after a period of intense online medical research regarding a low-titer ANA result, the patient experienced an acute, severe sympathetic nervous system response. Symptoms include sudden tachycardia, hyperventilation, and a subjective sense of crushing chest pressure. Patient fears impending death from a major organ crisis. Denies pain radiating to the jaw/arm, true syncope, or coughing up blood.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute health anxiety driving severe physiological distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 108 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, extremely anxious, hyperventilating, sitting rigidly and clutching the chest.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a rapid, regular rhythm (sinus tachycardia) without murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally with excellent air entry; no crackles or wheeze. Peripheral extremities are well-perfused. History confirms low-titer ANA, mild fatigue, transient Raynaud's, does not progress to distinct syndrome. Chest wall is mildly tender to palpation (costochondritis/muscle tension) but pain does not worsen with exertion.
Environmental Observations: Computer open to heavy medical articles. Safe physical environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current cardiac or immunosuppressive medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Health Anxiety and Panic Attack secondary to Undifferentiated Connective Tissue Disease (Mild ANA Positive) - Status: Stable without cardiopulmonary pathology
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia and tightness directly correlating with an acute psychological panic trigger, regular rhythm, and oxygen saturation maintained at 100%.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lung fields, normal oxygenation on room air, and lack of unilateral leg swelling or recent immobilization.

Acute Lupus Pericarditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of auscultated friction rub, and pain that does not classically change with leaning forward or breathing deeply.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe autonomic overactivity, tachycardia, and subjective chest tightness, highly mimicking an acute cardiac or pulmonary event. Triage appropriately mandated immediate physical evaluation to rule out a life-threatening cardiopulmonary emergency. Bedside evaluation firmly established normal oxygenation, clear lungs, and a regular heart rhythm, definitively confirming the symptoms were an acute panic attack triggered by medical hypervigilance over a benign, low-level autoimmune finding. The immediate provision of clinical reassurance safely aborted the panic attack, entirely preventing an unnecessary emergency department surge that could not have waited until morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the physical stability of the heart and lungs. Instructed on grounded box-breathing techniques to resolve hyperventilation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Shut down the computer immediately and perform the 4-7-8 breathing exercise (inhale 4s, hold 7s, exhale 8s) for five minutes. By stopping the research and consciously controlling your breath, you will manually override your body's fight-or-flight response, instantly proving that your heart and lungs are healthy and capable.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 1-2 weeks to discuss the benign nature of UCTD and manage health anxiety.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you experience severe, crushing chest pain that radiates to your left arm or jaw, if you begin coughing up blood, or if you feel faint and lose consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the exact nature of the autoimmune profile; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: A low-titer ANA is extremely common and often means nothing serious. Your condition is essentially a mild autoimmune quirk that rarely progresses to any severe disease. Your organs are perfectly safe tonight.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your shortness of breath persists even when you are completely calm, resting, and not thinking about your health.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tightness in your chest is caused by the muscles between your ribs spasming from breathing too fast and being terrified. Your heart is beating fast purely from adrenaline.
Actionable Steps: Stop reading medical forums online. Drink a glass of cold water and engage in a distracting, non-medical activity until you feel sleepy.

